let's get more on the tory leadership  race from a supporter of liz trust i'm  pleased to say we're joined by the  former northern ireland secretary  brandon lewis brenda lewis good morning  to you thank you very very much for  coming in good morning so  let's just pick up first of all on those  polls that we saw overnight a huge  boost for liz trust in the campaign but  david davis thinks that we can't trust  those polls because they're all a bit  different  well dave is right in the sense of any  poll is a snapshot in time and this is  quite a big poll of party members and it  builds on the work they did a couple of  weeks ago that gave it is a good lead as  well but the reality is there's only one  poll that counts and that's the one  where the members actually vote and  we'll get the results of that in  september so it's important for all of  us we've got to keep working  keep talking to members and this is  getting around the country talking to  members as well as the hustings and the  events we've got going on to secure  those votes for liz to become the next  leader of the conservative unionist  party and as a result the next prime  minister how damaging was that road back  on the regional papers do you think  i i don't think it was look what liz did  yesterday which was very clear we're not  taking that part of the policy forward  it's part of a bigger package obviously  she was talking about dealing with  whitehall waste which i think people do  want to see us uh deal with we've seen  with the rishis campaign as well he's  had like nine different things now where  he's flip-flopped around i'm pleased to  see he's now supporting the north island  protocol and i'm pleased to see he  believes in a lower tax economy although  i don't think we should be waiting seven  years for that at least wants to get on  with it as quickly as possible and put  more money in people's pockets in the  first seven weeks  let's have a look at what was said about  this idea because it was described as  willful misrepresentation  this is what was said a couple of days  ago  trust would introduce regional papers  tailoring pay to the cost of living  where civil servants actually work this  could save 8.8 billion pounds per year  potential savings and the system were to  be adopted for all public sector workers  in the long term so how did we  misrepresent that when we put that as  jacob rees-mogg was sitting there  yesterday and he defended it and then an  hour or so later it was entirely  scrapped well what was the  misrepresentation we saw some people in  other parts on the the other side of the  campaign arguing that this was about  reducing pay for the civil servants and  the public sector works who've done such  brilliant work over the last and it's  not about reducing no no it's never been  about that look what that's outlining is  if you were to extrapolate this out of  cross then yes you could see those kind  of savings but this was actually always  about look how you look at what you do  in new contracts and new people come  into the civil service but as i say  importantly this has been very clear as  she was yesterday pretty quickly after  that she's not taking this part of that  policy forward at all halfway to  defending it and i'm saying it was a  good idea the idea that someone getting  who's a nurse working in the south of  england could get paid more than a nurse  working up in the north you were very  close to saying that was a good idea  what the whole package was about  yesterday was dealing with whitehall  waste and actually making sure we're  spending this every penny the government  spends is taxpayers money we need to  make sure we spend that efficiently and  effectively  what was outlined there was what you  could save if you were doing this across  the board but that was never the case  and this was actually really clear about  this yesterday herself this was about  actually looking at what you could do  with new people coming into the civil  service but she's being we're not going  to do that we're not taking that part of  it forward what she wants to focus on  though is dealing with whitehall waste  as part of getting economic growth which  means we can actually reduce taxes put  more money in people's pockets and  deliver for people across the country  and of course importantly people talked  about this linked to leveling up the  best way we get leveling up is investing  across the country particularly in  education area liz has got experience of  and has got a very strong set of  policies to deliver on for people in the  future does it speak to questions around  liz truster's judgment that she'd even  put this policy forward and it it's not  like it's a brand new idea my  understanding is is one of liz truster's  ideas from about four years ago and a  time when people are going through a  many people feel like they're going  through a real terms pay cut we have a  cost of living crisis the idea that you  can be paid differently in different  parts of the country doing the same job  will not be a very good vote whenever  well as i say liz has been clear but  that's not something she's going to be  doing look what we have what we have  seen but she still put it she ran and  she's all put it forward and as i say  she's also been very one of the things  you've seen with liz trust both in her  jobs in government but actually over the  last few weeks as well through this  leadership campaign is somebody who's  looking at how do we improve our country  how do we grow our economy create more  jobs create more opportunity for people  improve education level up across the  country and she's somebody who even when  things are difficult will get on and get  things done she's done that as a foreign  secretary i've stood side by side with  her defending the people of northern  ireland against the pressure from the eu  she was resolute on that and we've seen  the northern ireland protocol bill go  through one of the things i think is  slightly odd that i see from uh  competitor camp if you like is they keep  flip-flopping around under like nine  different things at the moment and  particularly on the protocol where i've  seen them sort of argue against it and  now i've come and supported nine  different things  on tax on on on the economy um on the  northern protocol bill on china on a  whole range of things there are areas  where they've said one thing then done  another what has been clear released is  consistently issued somebody when she  outlines something that she wants to do  even when it's difficult again the  northern ireland protocol bill is  controversial it's been challenging with  the eu but she has been determined to do  the right things even when it's  difficult she will go out and have that  conversation and drive something forward  to deliver the right thing for the  people of the uk but all of you guys  flip-flopping around throwing things at  the wall and seeing which will stick i  mean over the weekend there was all this  talk about education reformer making  sure that every single student would get  who got a stars would get an interview  at oxbridge it seems to have quietly  been knocked down now that unions and uh  universities don't seem happy about it  so all of you are trying to throw  policies at the wall to see which ones  will stick aren't you some of them  sticking some of them down this one  obviously didn't well there's a couple  of things to say to that first of all  obviously during the leadership campaign  obviously the both the candidates are  out there talking about the kind of  things they would want to do as prime  minister liz has also been very open  about the fact we've got a manifesto  from 2019 to deliver so when she's  talking about the economic and the tax  benefits she wants to bring to people  into companies that's in the context of  delivering the manifesto but in the  context of where we are today with the  economic challenges we have but i think  the the education package that we  outlined at the weekend including the  university one is a really good one in  that it delivers on aspiration and says  to people if you're good enough you'll  have the opportunities that you want to  have you won't be restricted by where  you're from or your background and  actually i've seen the benefit of this  in my own constituency where the  improvement that we've seen in our  schools through academies and through  free schools has been utterly phenomenal  and transformational for students now  able to take advantage of the jobs that  coming through and that's what liz wants  to deliver on and that's what that  package of the weekend absolutely  outlines how useful to keep in the union  together is describe a nuclear surgeon  as an attention seeker who should be  ignored well i think actually keeping  the union together there's again an area  where liz trust has got huge credentials  and just on that particular issue i  think she i absolutely back her up on  this we had a yeah we had we had a  referee  in terms of looking at the referendum  and the independence or part of the  union for scotland we had a referendum  now we're all a few years older but it's  not been a generational change just yet  and that was always clear and the smp  were that that was a once in a  generation that decision has been made  what we should all be focused on  including the snp is how we improve the  lives of people in scotland they're  failing them on education i would argue  the health service and other areas of  the economy as well what liz wants to do  is make sure everybody across the uk and  every part of the uk gets better  education opportunities to take part in  a better and stronger economy in the  future let me get a thought from you on  the rwanda policy i think you'll have  seen the reporting this morning that the  the court has said that it cannot be  there can be no flights going until at  least november pretty patel home  secretary the moment says that she's  disappointed by that well let's trust  continue with that policy because we had  700 people crossing the channel  yesterday look yes i haven't seen the  details this morning yet actually but  look she will be continuing that policy  somebody's been in the immigration  ministry and spent a few years my life  in the home office in various jobs  dealing with border security i think it  is right that we are doing everything we  can to break the business model of the  those people are perpetrating modern  slavery people trafficking we've seen  reports even in the media as well about  already how it's having an impact for  some although we saw these record  numbers this week which i think  reinforce and remind us there's still  work to do and we've got to make sure  that we're delivering on that kind of  policy to break this business model of  the people traffickers that are putting  people's lives at risk it also means of  course the other side of this is when  people do come to this country and do it  through a legal rule we're able to give  them the support that they need to  integrate and be an important part in  our economy in our in our civilized you  know civil society in a way that they  want to be so illegal immigration  doesn't work because it perpetrates more  crime it puts lives at risk it means  that if people do get here they then  don't get the support that they need has  always been there aren't enough legal  routes will this trust expand legal  routes for people when they come back  actually i think and somebody as i say  is big immigration minister we have got  very clear legal routes for both asylum  seekers for refugees for people who want  to migrate to the uk we've got to focus  on making sure that they are there for  people who deliver on them but the best  way you do that is to be clear that you  have to take a legal route we are not  able we shouldn't be accepting illegal  routes into the country and we've got to  continue to crack down on that in every  way that we can okay brandon let's have  to leave it there thank you very much  for being with us good morning to you  thank you

